<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
this letter makes some reader comments seem wise.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:ramseyalrikabi@yahoo.com">ralrika</a>
	

 at November 20,2007 23:46
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2867"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I don't have a problem with the decision to hold her name. There are decent arguments for that. Obviously, there are also arguments for naming her or  they wouldn't have spent time looking for her.<br />
My problem with this is the tone. Seems like he's trying to build a distinction: Us - The Noble Media - and You - The Leering, Lurid, Sleeze-Hungry Reader.<br />
Talking down to readers seems like a bad idea to me.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:doyle.m.murphy@gmail.com">Doyle</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 08:46
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2868"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
Ralrika: this letter makes some reader comments seem wise. <br />
<br />
So you don't agree with the decision? Why?<br />
It made sense to me. I recently covered a DWI, where an NYPD cop was behind the wheel. He was married and had a woman in the car. She was not being charged. No reason to mention her name in that story. Similar to this one, no?     
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		sully 	

 at November 21,2007 09:00
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2869"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I'm not saying I  don't necessarily agree with the decision. <br />
<br />
It's the letter. The letter is obnoxious. <br />
<br />
"A lot of people are curious about the woman, but you're going to just have to stay that way."<br />
<br />
*Suck it, reader.<br />
<br />
"It took a bit of research, and a lucky tip, but our reporters were able to nail down quite a bit of information about the 24-year-old woman -- where she lives, where she went to school, even what her interests are." <br />
<br />
*Hey! You know of the  MySpace. Nice.<br />
<br />
"People say the era of the editor as gatekeeper of the news is over.<br />
Well, fine. Knock yourselves out on Google, folks"<br />
<br />
*Will do!<br />
<br />
"Absent information we don't now have, it's hard to imagine anyone would gain much of value from our publication of information about Sweeney's passenger."<br />
<br />
And know that I love newspapers, but there is a lot of stuff in the papers without much "value." For instance, Page 6? Love it. Sure don't need it.<br />
<br />
It's just one long condescension. Could have, should've, been done in a short note to the reader. Clean, simple.  <br />
<br />
You know, maybe I would name her...until, of course, the exec editor had to go and make a big deal about the whole thing. Now she is newsworthy only because Rex won't name her.<br />
<br />
The woman in Sully's story was in the car with an NYPD cop. So? He's what? One of 20,000 or something. An NYPD cop does not have the same public figure status as Sweeney. Period. <br />
<br />
Monica Lewinsky is known not because she blew someone's husband at his office. She blew Bill Clinton at the White House. That's why we know her name (and, ok, she did creepily save that dress...so...bad example, I suppose)<br />
<br />
Record folks: What if it was Ed Diana? Imagine he's left office.<br />
<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:ramseyalrikabi@yahoo.com">ralrika</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 10:13
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2870"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I would name her.<br />
<br />
You don't want your name in a headline? Don't climb onto the lap of an ex-congressman who might be loaded while he's driving. Don't want to make stupid decisions? Don't drink so much!<br />
<br />
(Those last two sentences are slightly hypocritical. If they still let newspapermen drink on the job there would be a flask in my desk drawer, believe me.)<br />
<br />
I actually like the note, though, I  don't think I would word it like that. I'm tired of taking crap from people outside of our craft who don't know what they are talking about and who seem far more arrogant than they accuse us of being. And I'm tired of newsroom leadership kowtowing to them.<br />
<br />
In this age, news organizations need to take actions that distinguish themselves from all the muck and crap that is classified as news and infotainment. Rather than being a luddite clinging to old ways and attitudes, I see Smith as a guy distinguishing himself from the competition, TV news in particular. <br />
<br />
I don't necessarily agree with his decision. But the strategy is sound.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:andrewnelson281@hotmail.com">Andrew</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 12:38
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2871"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I'm on the fence about whether withholding her name was the right or wrong decision. But I like that the newspaper took the time to think about it and weigh the benefits with the potential effect on the woman; something papers and media should probably do more often. <br />
<br />
We operate under this blanket need and right to know, but it may be an unpopular truth in the journalism industry that perhaps we don't always need to know.<br />
<br />
Ramsey - as far as the tone of the column, I agree that some may see it as condescending, &#8216;na na, I know this but won&#8217;t tell you.&#8217; But I think more readers will see it, and appreciate it, as a newspaper weighing the pros and cons of respecting someone's privacy rather than rushing to print. And having made its decision the paper could have not talked about it but instead chose to be open about it. That&#8217;s a good thing.<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:alrooj@gmail.com">Raja</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 13:24
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2872"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
sure, yes, a newspaper should put its reasons out there. got no problem with that.<br />
<br />
i'm just hung up on the tacky letter.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:ramseyalrikabi@yahoo.com">ralrika</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 13:43
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2873"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
...and i don't know why i'm so bothered.<br />
 sorry, everyone. it's really my own problem that i read it last night and can't get it out of my head.<br />
<br />
maybe i listened to too much Media Project on WAMC back in the day
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		ralrika	

 at November 21,2007 13:49
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2874"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
Yes, Alan Chartock has been known to traumatize a many starry-eyed reporters.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:alrooj@gmail.com">Raja</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 14:04
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2876"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I'm with you, Ramsey. Give me a fracking break with that highfalutin editor's note. Print the woman's name or do not print the woman's name. Writing that column actually makes her more newsworthy now. <br />
<br />
I vote for printing the name. I'm not a fan of withholding information unless there is a vital reason for it. We spend way too much time worrying over readers' tender sensibilities and/or lurid impulses. <br />
<br />
Seems like an easy addition, to me.<br />
<br />
"Also in the car with the ex-congressman was Holly Golightly, 24, of Manhattan. Golightly declined repeated requests to discuss the incident.<br />
According to her myspace page, Golightly came to Manhattan from a small town in Oklahoma, has a cat she adores and is   neighbors with the guy who would later star on the A-Team."<br />
<br />
Then you can make the decision whether to run another story going into the woman's background. <br />
<br />
She's in a car with a drunk former congressman. That's news.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:zackmcm@yahoo.com">zack</a>
	

 at November 21,2007 17:47
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2885"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
Amen, Zack. I think that's the right way to play it, and if editors thought otherwise, a simple note about their reasoning would suffice. I like when newspapers explain their thought processes to readers, but the TU buried a sound argument under an obnoxious lede. I'm not saying Rex Smith is a douche, but I feel like he's making the whole newspaper smell like one.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:ajames@th-record.com">Seamus</a>
	

 at November 23,2007 10:56
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2889"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
I agree with ralrika.<br />
And I disagree with the decision not to publish her name. You're bending over backwards to protect a public figure.<br />
<br />
I had the rare experience of being arrested for dwi in my newspaper's coverage area while I was a reporter there. my name, my age, my job, the circumstances of my arrest, all of it, was published. I was embarassed of course. But fair play...<br />
<br />
Those details are also of course available on the arrest report. Which is public. Any reader of the albany paper could likely pull that name off the arrest sheet. That makes the we-know-and-you-don't crap that much more obnoxious.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:jsdoherty3@gmail.com">snake</a>
	

 at November 25,2007 12:40
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2890"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
Zack and Snake's reasoning to print this woman's name both miss the point. 1) It's not the lurid interests of the readers the editors were thinking about, it was the woman's welfare. And 2) Fair play makes sense only when the players are newsworthy. This woman herself was not newsworthy. She held no positions of power and did not seek the spotlight. Her only mistake was shacking up with an ex-Congressman (whose name was printed). As for reporters, WE ARE VERY FAIR PLAY. If we're doing our jobs right, we not only help set the agenda of public debate (what gets talked about) but the tone of these debates. You can whine all you want about the pay and the constant criticism about what we do, but people respect us, even fear us a little. We are held to the same standards as cops, firefighters and teachers. And in some cases, we are held to an even higher standard.  Bad comparison.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		Sully	

 at November 26,2007 00:03
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2900"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
And rightly so. But the irony in all of this, as was pointed out earlier, is it would take just one blogger to go down to the cop shop/DPS building, get the report, and put her name online. Bang. Like that. <br />
<br />
While the editor encourages us to "knock (ourselves) out on Google," he'll soon find that, yes, people are going to Google, or that blog, to get the information. And right there, all of our examples of fancy writing get the old defenestration. <br />
<br />
It's public record. It's going to get out. I'd like to agree with the paper -- in theory, I DO agree -- but in practice, people will want the news. <br />
<br />
So fine. Disassociate yourself from the vulgar, lurid masses itching to see nakie pictures of pretty people on the picture-screen. But if we continue to lift our noses higher and higher, I fear we risk drowning when the roof leaks. 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:nigel.duara@gmail.com">Nigel</a>
	

 at November 28,2007 22:30
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2906"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Privacy
</h4>

<p>
It's simply about being righteous, or the image in which we are supposed to do our jobs. And yes, sometimes it is just "image." Newspapers are supposed to do what?  "Comfort the afflicted and afflict the comfortable." We are supposed to be just. If we give in to the belief that getting the story is more important than the people we cover, then we will have lost our sense of purpose.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		sully 	

 at November 29,2007 20:04
</div>
